 afghanistan and not to have  television films such as you've shown  everybody showing what pitiful condition  that they are in um is there pride yes  pride in our soldiers is there pride in  what our government and international  governments are doing not at all  yeah i'm watching stuart's report this  morning before coming on the program  it's difficult not to to have been moved  by but just talk a little bit more then  about your experience in country the the  assistance that the british did give  during that 20-year period i was on the  ground a few times with with british  forces saw the hearts and minds efforts  saw the education of children in a way  which hadn't had never been done before  all of that progress it would seem has  now been rolled back by a taliban that  resembles the taliban of 2001.  yes i think we all have to acknowledge  that the taliban are repressive but  again it comes back to the question um  we went to afghanistan to help the  people of afghanistan now we're making  films watching the people of afghanistan  suffer yes we may not like the taliban  we may not like their attitude i  certainly don't towards women and girls  and stopping girls getting to secondary  education but  just because we don't like their human  rights record and of course it's a big  issue but because we don't like their  human rights record and the number of  things that they're doing we're  withholding aid from the people of that  country such that babies are dying  people are starving and  it's not for want of food in the country  it's for want of money to be able to buy  it we have starved the place of cash  in an economy that was made up by about  80 percent of international aid forming  the cash flow of that country so we  could change this we could stop this we  actually could help the people of  afghanistan and i believe that um  diplomats and statesmen should be  thinking seriously about starting aid  again to afghanistan and using that aid  to leverage  on the taliban to get them to change  some of the  attitudes that they take particularly  towards women and girls and girls  secondary education  just to be clear though about what  you're saying there lord danette i mean  given the fact that 60 of of afghanistan  is currently suffering from malnutrition  their gold reserves are still frozen  there has been a stop to aid i mean are  we in the west then essentially  responsible perhaps even more  responsible than the taliban for what  stewart was seeing in the hospitals  well put it bluntly  the american decision initially by  donald trump and then agreed by joe  biden  that the americans would leave  afghanistan by august last year which  was a unilateral decision about which we  were not consulted and other partners  were not consulted that decision  directly led to the collapse of the  afghan national army the afghan national  police and allowed the taliban to take  over the country  that having happened  the west has said we don't like the  taliban so we're going to cut off aid to  this country and having cut off aid to  afghanistan that is why babies are dying  in hospitals that is why people are  starving up and down the country now  is that right  as i said before and we spent 20 years  building up  afghanistan afghanistan society we spent  20 years helping the people why should  we suddenly stop helping the people now  because we don't like the taliban  frankly  yes their human rights record is not  good they have stopped second girls  going to secondary school but is that  the right price to pay for the majority  of afghan people to be starving and  babies to be dying i don't think so i  think we've got this terribly wrong and  governments need to wake up start the  flow of aid again and  give the afghan people for whom we tried  to help them for 20 years give them the  prospect of a future while bearing down  on the taliban to say come on you  actually got to behave like a civilized  country a civilized government or  otherwise um you'll never take your  proper place in the world  it's part of the situation that we're  seeing at the moment perhaps that much  that many of the world's eyes are  elsewhere in fact afghanistan is a topic  rarely discussed around the dinner  tables these days i mean last august you  were speaking to us on the channel you  said something terrible will happen  elsewhere the world's media will move on  and this is when the taliban will  enforce their harsh regime i mean did  that happen in terms of ukraine  well of course it did um the disasters  emergency committee for example launched  an appeal this time last year to raise  funds for afghanistan but quite quickly  come february this year  they launched an appeal for ukraine and  that  raised over 300 million pounds um  and it's not as if it's ukraine that's  actually pushed afghanistan off the news  now the cost of living crisis and the  and the energy uh cost increases have  always pushed ukraine off the news we  are very short-termist in terms of our  of our intervention and it comes back to  my basic point we spent 20 years trying  to build up afghan society and help  afghan people why do we walk away now  and watch films a year on such as the  one that you're showing people this  morning of babies dying and people  starving frankly we've got to be  prepared to have longer engagements in  this country if we think human life is  worth preserving otherwise we'll just  watch them all perish
 

